WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
A convincing circuit diagram is only a starting

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:03.840
point.

3
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.220
This workflow combines an AI agent, schematic editing,

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.200
and circuit simulation, so a design

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.740
proposal can be examined against its requirements.

6
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.620
The agent uses the design requirements to propose

7
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.720
a circuit topology.

8
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.460
KiCad, through the Konnect interface, supports schematic editing

9
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.040
and checks.

10
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.780
The simulation tool then evaluates performance measures including

11
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.180
amplifier gain and bandwidth.

12
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.620
Compare the simulated results with the design specifications.

13
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.920
Check gain and bandwidth against their targets, and

14
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.200
keep the underlying outputs.

15
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.640
If evidence is missing or a requirement is

16
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.540
not met, the design has not passed.

17
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.200
Without supporting evidence, the design has not passed.

18
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.180
The goal is a traceable workflow.

19
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.660
propose a design, check the schematic, run the

20
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.840
simulation, and review the results before accepting the

21
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.180
design.

22
00:00:57.914 --> 00:01:01.878
Before asking AI to design a circuit, define
success.

23
00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:04.982
A simulation that runs is only a starting
point.

24
00:01:05.243 --> 00:01:07.665
The final verdict needs measured results.

25
00:01:08.846 --> 00:01:10.668
Set the gain target at ten,

26
00:01:11.028 --> 00:01:12.649
with a five percent tolerance.

27
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.931
Acceptable gain is nine point five

28
00:01:14.931 --> 00:01:16.792
to ten point five volts per volt.

29
00:01:17.113 --> 00:01:19.174
Require a bandwidth of at least

30
00:01:19.174 --> 00:01:20.615
one hundred fifty kilohertz,

31
00:01:20.976 --> 00:01:23.077
measured at the first three-decibel crossing

32
00:01:23.077 --> 00:01:25.059
from the simulated low-frequency gain.

33
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.842
Keep the simulation assumptions explicit.

34
00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:31.464
The example uses an open-loop gain

35
00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:32.885
of one hundred thousand

36
00:01:33.165 --> 00:01:35.447
and a single-pole gain-bandwidth product

37
00:01:35.547 --> 00:01:36.687
of one megahertz.

38
00:01:37.048 --> 00:01:39.469
Then sweep from ten hertz to ten megahertz,

39
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.511
with a one-volt AC input.

40
00:01:41.631 --> 00:01:44.313
Check whether those assumptions can support
the targets.

41
00:01:45.353 --> 00:01:47.774
Check the circuit before trusting the verdict.

42
00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:49.535
Inspect the schematic and model,

43
00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:51.616
including the electrical rules check.

44
00:01:52.016 --> 00:01:53.617
Then measure gain and bandwidth

45
00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:55.077
from the simulator's output.

46
00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:57.818
Compare both with the acceptance criteria

47
00:01:57.818 --> 00:01:59.919
before declaring the design successful.

48
00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:05.133
A reliable AI circuit workflow gives each

49
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:06.377
tool a clear job.

50
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.422
DeepSeek and OpenCode help propose circuit
designs,

51
00:02:10.582 --> 00:02:13.163
select components, and decide what to
investigate

52
00:02:13.163 --> 00:02:15.344
next. Their role is to coordinate the

53
00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:18.586
workflow. A convincing explanation still
needs evidence

54
00:02:18.586 --> 00:02:19.866
from the engineering tools.

55
00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:23.068
MCP provides the tool interface.

56
00:02:23.348 --> 00:02:25.909
The agent discovers available actions,
supplies their

57
00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:28.771
parameters, and receives results or error
messages.

58
00:02:28.991 --> 00:02:31.432
That makes the handoff inspectable,
instead of

59
00:02:31.432 --> 00:02:33.193
hiding it inside a conversation.

60
00:02:34.293 --> 00:02:37.695
KiCad, connected through Konnect in this
workflow,

61
00:02:37.815 --> 00:02:40.116
handles the schematic and connectivity
checks.

62
00:02:40.436 --> 00:02:42.377
It also provides the circuit description
for

63
00:02:42.377 --> 00:02:45.318
simulation. Passing an electrical rules
check does

64
00:02:45.318 --> 00:02:47.039
not prove that an amplifier meets its

65
00:02:47.039 --> 00:02:48.239
performance requirements.

66
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.942
ngspice performs the numerical circuit
analysis.

67
00:02:53.483 --> 00:02:55.884
An AC sweep can show gain across

68
00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:58.526
frequency and help assess bandwidth.

69
00:02:59.126 --> 00:03:01.608
Those results depend on the netlist,
device

70
00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:03.349
models, and analysis settings.

71
00:03:03.929 --> 00:03:06.851
Model validity, convergence, and
performance against the

72
00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:09.273
design requirements remain separate
checks.

73
00:03:10.654 --> 00:03:12.295
Let AI coordinate.

74
00:03:12.795 --> 00:03:14.737
Let engineering tools calculate.

75
00:03:15.197 --> 00:03:17.899
Keep the inputs, checks, and results
traceable.

76
00:03:20.202 --> 00:03:22.107
Simulation is just the beginning

77
00:03:22.108 --> 00:03:23.171
of board design.

78
00:03:24.053 --> 00:03:26.014
Before layout, check the circuit

79
00:03:26.014 --> 00:03:28.735
against its required gain and bandwidth

80
00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:30.176
in ngspice.

81
00:03:30.656 --> 00:03:32.697
Keep the simulation log, netlist,

82
00:03:32.717 --> 00:03:33.778
and model checksum.

83
00:03:33.938 --> 00:03:35.759
Those records preserve the evidence

84
00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:36.719
behind the result.

85
00:03:37.521 --> 00:03:40.184
Next, capture the production schematic.

86
00:03:40.625 --> 00:03:43.108
Use the real operational amplifier part,

87
00:03:43.388 --> 00:03:44.790
appropriate power supplies,

88
00:03:45.071 --> 00:03:46.412
decoupling capacitors,

89
00:03:46.613 --> 00:03:47.974
and the feedback connections.

90
00:03:48.215 --> 00:03:50.258
The physical implementation needs details

91
00:03:50.258 --> 00:03:51.419
beyond the simulation.

92
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.921
Then move into KiCad

93
00:03:53.921 --> 00:03:55.982
for component placement and routing.

94
00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:58.263
Autorouting can be an optional part

95
00:03:58.263 --> 00:03:59.263
of that workflow.

96
00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:00.924
Review the resulting layout.

97
00:04:00.924 --> 00:04:03.346
Identify and verify any separately named

98
00:04:03.346 --> 00:04:05.026
routing tool before relying on it.

99
00:04:05.827 --> 00:04:08.190
Finally, run the board design rule check.

100
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.571
Review noise,

101
00:04:09.771 --> 00:04:11.713
how current returns through the board,

102
00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.034
signal coupling,

103
00:04:13.055 --> 00:04:14.716
and manufacturing requirements.

104
00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:16.538
Use the rule check alongside

105
00:04:16.538 --> 00:04:18.080
the wider engineering review.

106
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.461
AI can assist with
planning and automation.

107
00:04:21.642 --> 00:04:23.322
Use each tool for its purpose.

108
00:04:23.382 --> 00:04:24.823
The assistant coordinates.

109
00:04:24.983 --> 00:04:27.364
ngspice measures circuit behavior,

110
00:04:27.364 --> 00:04:29.325
and KiCad supports the board checks.

111
00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:31.486
Verification remains essential.
